<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/expert/attribution-conservation-and-forgery">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the forensic edge of Art History, and it leans on the ladder you have already
    climbed. "Renaissance and Baroque" (intermediate) supplies the contract documents and
    patronage that later become evidence; "Art beyond the West" (intermediate) supplies the
    context for the Benin Bronzes case; "Art market and museums" (advanced) supplies the price
    and institutional machinery that executes every attribution verdict.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain why attribution questions arise structurally — copies, workshops, delegated fabrication — and answer with the honest formulas ("Roman copy of", "workshop of");</li>
    <li>run the tribunal: weigh the eye, documents and instruments as independent witnesses, and state confidence in the field's graded wording;</li>
    <li>name the main laboratory instruments and exactly what each can and cannot testify to;</li>
    <li>diagnose forgery as an exploit of expectation, using van Meegeren, Hebborn and the Knoedler case as the pattern;</li>
    <li>apply conservation's ethics — minimum intervention, reversibility, documentation — to a fresco and to a protocol-based installation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Attribution, conservation and forgery are <strong>art history's laboratory and courtroom</strong>
    — where connoisseurship meets spectroscopy and every judgement carries legal, financial and
    ethical weight. It is the discipline's most falsifiable edge. The syllabus attaches this
    lesson to two content areas at once — <em>Ancient Mediterranean art</em> and <em>Global
    Contemporary</em> — because both raise the same questions at opposite ends of history: the
    ancient world produced systematic copying (Roman versions of Greek masterpieces) and
    workshop production that makes "by whom?" genuinely hard; the contemporary world produces
    assisted fabrication, editions and certificates that make it hard again. Between them sit
    the instruments, the forgers, and the conservators — three professions whose judgements the
    market, the courts and the museums execute.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the tribunal, the instruments, the forgers, the conservator oath" widget="equationStep" config={forgeryCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps are the lesson's cast in order of
    appearance. Step 1 is the tribunal with its three witnesses; step 2 lists the instruments
    and the single rule that organises them — the fraud must fool every instrument at once.
    Step 3 introduces the adversary who exploits not the instruments but the experts'
    expectations, and step 4 the profession whose interventions become part of the object's
    history. Watch the direction of travel: each step tightens the discipline from individual
    judgement toward accountable, documented process.
  </p>

  <h2>Cultural contexts: why attribution questions arise at all</h2>
  <p>
    The attribution problem is not a failure of scholarship; it is a property of how art was
    and is made. In the <strong>ancient Mediterranean</strong>, the master's name carried the
    work's value while the labour was distributed: Greek sculpture existed in bronze originals
    (now almost all lost — Praxiteles and Lysippos survive only through copies), and Rome
    industrialised reproduction, filling villas with marble versions of Greek classics whose
    fidelity varies from masterly to garbled; temple and tomb production ran through
    workshops with apprentices finishing the master's design. The question "is this a
    Praxiteles?" is therefore often malformed — the honest answers are "a Roman copy of a
    Greek original" or "workshop of", and a large part of classical scholarship is dating and
    placing those copies. In the <strong>global contemporary</strong>, the same structure
    reappears by choice: Warhol's Factory stamped silkscreens with assistants; Koons and
    Hirst commission fabrication from specialist firms (Hirst's animals in formaldehyde,
    Koons's stainless-steel balloon dogs — executed by others, signed by the artist); editions
    multiply authorised copies. When authorship is delegated <em>by design</em>, attribution
    becomes a legal-philosophical question — what is the work? the concept, the certificate,
    the artist's supervision? — which is why the discipline's answer tools (catalogues
    raisonnés, authentication committees, certificates) grew up in the market era.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> attribution is hard because authorship was structurally
    distributed — in antiquity by workshop, today by design. Before the instruments can speak,
    though, one prior question has to be answered for any object: what was it for?
  </p>

  <h2>Purpose and audience: what the object was for</h2>
  <p>
    Attribution and conservation decisions make no sense without the object's purpose, and the
    purposes across the two content areas structure everything. An ancient Greek <em>votive</em>
    or cult statue existed for the god, not the connoisseur: its value was ritual efficacy, its
    finish and repairs (meant to be seen at festival scale, painted brightly — the polychromy
    evidence now recovered by ultraviolet and Raman analysis) served devotion. A Roman villa
    copy served display and cultivation — a badge of Greek paideia — which is why accuracy to
    the original mattered less than the <em>type</em>. A medieval reliquary served the saint
    and the pilgrim; a Renaissance altarpiece served doctrine and patron simultaneously (the
    contract documents: pigment budgets — ultramarine priced like gold, specified by weight).
    The contemporary work's purposes: the market (the certificate of authenticity is the
    object's legal double), the institution (the museum's accession file is its biography),
    the discourse (the biennale argument), and — newly — conservation policy, since a
    performance or a chocolate-and-tarp installation (Felix Gonzalez-Torres's candy spills,
    replenished by instruction) exists precisely as a protocol rather than a thing. The
    expert's reflex: before judging an object, reconstruct what it was <em>for</em> — because
    forgeries exploit the purpose's expectations (a "lost Vermeer" for a market hungry for
    Vermeer) and conservators are bound by it (the altarpiece must keep working as an
    altarpiece, not as a chart of its restorations).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> purpose sets the frame in which attribution and conservation
    judgements make sense — and in which forgers aim. The next section follows the objects
    themselves as they move: ownership, removal, restitution, and the documents that carry the
    legal weight.
  </p>

  <h2>Interactions across cultures: provenance and the movement of objects</h2>
  <p>
    Attribution's legal twin is provenance: the documented history of an object's ownership
    and movement — and the two content areas both carry the heavy versions of that history.
    The ancient Mediterranean canon is entangled with empire and tourism: the <strong>Elgin
    Marbles</strong> (removed from the Parthenon 1801–12, purchased by the British
    government 1816; Greece's restitution claim continuous since) and the <strong>Benin
    Bronzes</strong> (looted in the 1897 British punitive expedition, dispersed to museums
    worldwide; German institutions formally transferred ownership to Nigeria in 2022) are the
    defining cases, and the method is documentary forensics: acquisition registers, export
    licences, shipping manifests, dealer archives. The twentieth century's layer is
    <strong>Nazi-era looting</strong> (1933–45): confiscations from Jewish collectors, forced
    sales, and the post-war restitution regimes (the 1998 Washington Principles committed
    institutions to identifying looted works — and the phrase "provenance gap 1933–45" is now
    a permanent field of scholarship, with databases and full-time researchers). Contemporary
    provenance adds the digital: blockchain-based chains of custody, Artist's Resale Rights
    royalties, and the forgery-adjacent problems of the unregulated ancient-antiquities trade
    (looted and smuggled "ancient" objects with fabricated histories). The methodological
    point: provenance research is source criticism — weighing documents for bias, gaps and
    forgery — the historian's oldest skill, applied where the stakes are legal restitution
    and eight-figure prices.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a provenance gap means the object is fake or looted." Gaps
    are the norm for old objects (private sales rarely generated paper before the modern
    era); what matters is the <em>pattern</em> — documents consistent with known dealers and
    exhibitions, or documents that surface conveniently with new ink. Attribution and
    provenance are probabilistic: the tribunal weighs convergent evidence and states its
    confidence, which is why reputable opinions are worded ("attributed to", "workshop of",
    "circle of") rather than stamped.
  </p>

  <h2>Theories and interpretations: connoisseurship and its critics</h2>
  <p>
    The theory of attribution has a distinguished, disputed history. <strong>Giovanni
    Morelli</strong> (1870s) proposed reading the artist's <em>unconscious habits</em> — ears,
    fingernails, drapery folds, the details a copyist does not bother to fake — as the
    diagnostic fingerprint; <strong>Bernard Berenson</strong> built a market empire on the
    eye (and was later shown to have certified forgeries for money — the field's permanent
    conflict-of-interest warning); the connoisseur's claim is that long immersion produces
    recognition no instrument replicates. The critics' case: the eye is trained on
    expectations and produces them (the van Meegeren disaster is the exhibit), expert
    opinion is notoriously unstable (the same painting reattributed across decades), and
    incentives corrupt. The modern settlement is the <strong>tribunal model</strong>: eye,
    documents and instruments as independent witnesses, weighed in committees (the
    Rembrandt Research Project's decades-long corpus revisions — downgrading and upgrading
    works by documented criteria — is the field's largest self-audit), with published
    reasoning. The contemporary strand adds machine assistance: stroke-level analysis models
    have attributed drawings and detected forgeries with high accuracy on test sets (the
    2019 Rutgers–Institut national d'histoire de l'art work on Picasso, Matisse and
    Modigliani drawings) — useful evidence, with documented failure modes, never an oracle;
    the tribunal remains human and accountable.
  </p>

  <h2>The instrument panel: what science can and cannot say</h2>
  <p>
    The laboratory's instruments, and exactly what each testifies to: <strong>X-radiography</strong>
    reveals density — paintings under paintings, structural repairs, the artist's revisions
    (pentimenti: a changed hand position argues for an original; absence of any revision
    argues, weakly, for a copy). <strong>Infrared reflectography</strong> maps carbon-based
    underdrawing beneath paint — the construction drawing, the sketch beneath the Rembrandt or
    the omission of one in a suspected copy. <strong>Pigment analysis</strong> (Raman
    spectroscopy, XRF scanning for elements) is the hardest chronological test: titanium white
    (a 20th-century pigment) cannot be in a genuine Rembrandt; Prussian blue postdates 1704;
    <em>anachronism is proof of forgery, but authenticity of every pigment proves only the
    materials, not the hand</em>. <strong>Dendrochronology</strong> dates panel supports by
    tree rings (a Baltic oak panel can be dated to a decade — but only the tree, not the
    carving). <strong>Radiocarbon dating</strong> dates organic supports (canvas fibres,
    parchment) with calibration curves and uncertainty ranges. <strong>Thermoluminescence</strong>
    dates the last firing of ceramics — the clock that exposed forged Etruscan terracottas.
    <strong>Canvas weave analysis</strong> matches canvases cut from the same bolt — the
    technique that recently connected dispersed paintings from a single roll. The synthesis
    the panel teaches: instruments bound the possible (no Vermeer painted after 1900), and
    the human tribunal judges within those bounds.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "science settles authenticity." It cannot, in principle:
    every instrument dates or characterises <em>materials</em>, and a perfect forgery uses
    correct materials (old canvases, historically accurate pigments — all purchasable). What
    science can do is catch the forger who was lazy, and bound the debate. Van Meegeren's
    forgeries fooled the 1937 eye precisely because he used period canvases and lapis-based
    blues — his exposure came from chemistry (he used phenol-formaldehyde resin to fake
    hardness, detectable in 1945) and confession, not from any instrument available to the
    experts he beat.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the tribunal now has its instruments — each bound to a specific
    testimony, all of them dateable and characterisable but none able to certify a hand. That
    limitation is exactly where the forgers strike, and the next section is their casebook.
  </p>

  <h2>Forgers and their tells: expectation is the vulnerability</h2>
  <p>
    The great forgeries are studies in the discipline's psychology. <strong>Han van
    Meegeren</strong> faked Vermeer's religious middle period — a period the leading Vermeer
    scholar Abraham Bredius had hypothesised and longed for; his <em>Supper at Emmaus</em>
    (1937) was acquired by Museum Boijmans for a very large sum and hailed in print by
    Bredius himself. The forgery worked because it flattered the scholars' expectation —
    van Meegeren painted the Vermeer the experts <em>wanted</em> — and because he solved the
    technical problems (genuine period canvas, hand-ground pigments, baked hardness, aged
    craquelure). Exposed in 1945 under a treason charge (selling national heritage to
    Göring), he demonstrated his method by painting a new "Vermeer" in custody — the
    demonstration was the evidence. <strong>Eric Hebborn</strong> forged old-master drawings
    (Piranesi, Corot, Poussin) with genuinely old paper, plausible provenance and mastered
    hands; his 1991 confession manual is now the forger's textbook and the curator's. The
    <strong>Knoedler scandal</strong> (2009–2011) is the modern case: a 165-year-old gallery
    sold roughly 80 million dollars of "abstract expressionist" paintings by Rothko, Pollock
    and de Kooning from a single undocumented "estate" — every one fake, created by one
    painter in Queens; the fraud survived because the story was coherent, the desire was
    strong, and nobody asked the simplest documentary questions. The discipline's lesson:
    forgeries succeed by exploiting expectation and gaps in verification, so the defence is
    institutional (independent committees, published provenance, instrument panels) rather
    than heroic.
  </p>

  <h2>Conservation ethics: how little is enough</h2>
  <p>
    Conservation is attribution's sister discipline — its interventions are interpretations
    that will be attributed by future scholars, which is why its ethics are codified
    (minimum intervention, reversibility, full documentation, respect for the object's
    history including its damages). The controversies are the course. The <strong>Sistine
    Chapel restoration</strong> (1980–94): solvent cleaning removed the grime and the
    "lacquinature" darkening glazes, returning the astonishing colours — and triggering the
    Beck–Vatican controversy over whether 16th-century a secco glazes (applied over the
    fresco) were original finishing layers or later dirt; the scientific publications
    defended the choices, and the debate remains the field's standard seminar on
    interpretation-as-cleaning. The doctrines: <strong>reversibility</strong> (future
    conservators can undo today's adhesive — the reason modern fills are designed to come
    out), <strong>distinct retouching</strong> (losses filled visibly at close range, not
    faked — tratteggio hatching), <strong>documentation</strong> (before, during, after —
    the object's treatment becomes part of its archive), and the honest limit that every
    cleaning decision is a theory of what the object was. The contemporary extension:
    conserving time-based and technological art (video obsolescence, migrating code,
    re-performing performances) has forced the field to conserve <em>intentions</em> and
    <em>instructions</em>, not materials — the object of conservation is now sometimes a
    protocol.
  </p>

  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "conservation restores the original." It never can and
    should not: the original state is unrecoverable (materials aged, the artist's own
    revisions are history), and each intervention is a new, documented interpretation that
    future generations will judge — the conservator's oath is that <em>future</em> generations
    outrank present audiences, which is why the discipline prizes restraint over spectacle.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why does an anachronistic pigment prove forgery, while every pigment proving correct establish only the materials?</li>
    <li>A "Vermeer" appears with a period canvas, period-correct pigments and a provenance gap from 1930 to 1960. Walk the tribunal's three witnesses: what does each say, and what wording should the verdict use?</li>
    <li>Why did van Meegeren's forgery succeed against the best experts of 1937 — and what institutional lesson did the discipline draw from it?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) An anachronism is proof because the material could not have
    existed — titanium white cannot be in a genuine Rembrandt. But correct materials are
    purchasable: old canvases and historically accurate pigments were exactly van Meegeren's
    kit. Science dates and characterises materials; it cannot certify a hand. (2) The
    instruments bound the possible — period canvas and pigments pass every chronological test,
    so their testimony is only "not excluded". The documents show a gap that is a normal shape
    for an old object, but the tribunal weighs the pattern: consistency with known dealers and
    exhibitions, or papers that surface conveniently with new ink. The eye reads style against
    expectation — and expectation is precisely the vulnerability. The verdict stays
    probabilistic: "attributed to", "workshop of" — worded, never stamped. (3) Because it
    painted the Vermeer the experts wanted: Bredius had hypothesised a religious middle period,
    and the forgery flattered that expectation while solving the technical problems. The lesson
    the discipline drew: the vulnerability is psychological, so the defence is institutional —
    independent committees, published provenance, instrument panels — rather than heroic
    connoisseurship.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Attribution is hard by design: Roman copies, workshop production and delegated fabrication distribute authorship structurally.</li>
    <li>The tribunal model weighs three independent witnesses — eye, documents, instruments — and states confidence in graded wording.</li>
    <li>Instruments bound the possible: anachronism proves forgery; correct materials prove only the materials.</li>
    <li>Provenance research is source criticism where the stakes are legal restitution and eight-figure prices.</li>
    <li>Forgeries exploit expectation — van Meegeren painted the Vermeer scholars wanted; the Knoedler fraud survived on a coherent story.</li>
    <li>Conservation never restores the original: minimum intervention, reversibility, documentation — future generations outrank present audiences.</li>
    <li>Time-based art has turned conservation's object into intentions and instructions — sometimes a protocol, not a thing.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling expert lesson "Art historiography and method" supplies the wider frame this
    chapter keeps invoking: source criticism, convergent evidence and the standing question set
    that keeps any attribution accountable. The advanced lesson "Art market and museums"
    develops the price and institutional machinery that executes the tribunal's verdicts —
    where an upgraded attribution and a downgraded one are measured in factors, not points.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The Salvator Mundi problem.</strong> The Leonardo-attribution case of the century:
    a heavily damaged, much-overpainted Christ purchased in 2005 for about 1,175 dollars,
    attributed to Leonardo after restoration, sold in 2017 for 450 million dollars — with
    dissenting scholars (a "workshop" attribution; a Leonardo-invention-executed-by-pupils
    hypothesis) documented on both sides. It is the whole lesson in one object: technical
    evidence (extensive damage and repainting limit every instrument), connoisseurship
    divided, provenance partially traceable (1649 English royal inventory onward), and a
    market price that then made re-attribution commercially catastrophic. The case is
    studied less for its verdict than for what it shows: attribution under high stakes is a
    social process with incentives, not a laboratory report.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Authentication committees and their limits.</strong> The Warhol Foundation's
    authentication board (1995–2012) was disbanded after lawsuits — the legal exposure of
    <em>saying no</em> killed the institution meant to police the fakes; similar pressures
    shaped other artist foundations. The economics are structural: authentication opinions
    move prices by factors, so every opinion is worth suing over. The field's responses:
    catalogues raisonnés maintained by scholars insulated from the market, standardised
    expert-witness norms, and emerging technical infrastructure (blockchain provenance,
    forensic imaging standards). The open question — who authenticates the authenticators —
    remains the discipline's live governance problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The AI frontier.</strong> Stroke-level and statistical models now enter the
    tribunal as a fourth witness: trained on documented corpora, they attribute, date and
    flag anomalies with published accuracy — and published failure modes (distribution shift:
    a model trained on authenticated works inherits the corpus's biases; adversarial
    sensitivity). The mature position mirrors the machine-learning evaluation chapter: the
    tool is evidence with a stated error rate, not an oracle; its value is greatest exactly
    where the eye is weakest (uniform statistics across thousands of strokes), and its
    testimony must be auditable. The tribunal stays human, because the tribunal's verdicts
    are legal acts.
  </p>

